Ethereum is a decentralized world computer that executes smart contracts and maintains a shared global state using blockchain technology.
Unlike Bitcoin, which mainly focuses on digital payments, Ethereum is a general-purpose programmable blockchain.
Ethereum uses ether (ETH) to pay for computation and transaction fees on the network.
Ethereum transitioned from Proof of Work (PoW) to Proof of Stake (PoS) during The Merge in 2022, improving energy efficiency and scalability.
A blockchain consists of components such as P2P networking, transactions, consensus rules, state machines, blocks, incentives, and clients.
Ethereum's history began in 2013, when Vitalik Buterin proposed a blockchain capable of running programmable smart contracts. The network officially launched in July 2015.
Ethereum evolved through stages: Frontier, Homestead, Metropolis, and Serenity, with Serenity including upgrades like The Merge, The Surge, and The Verge.
The Ethereum Virtual Machine (EVM) executes smart contracts, making Ethereum Turing complete, meaning it can run any computable program.
To prevent infinite loops and resource abuse, Ethereum uses gas, which limits the computational resources a transaction can consume.
Ethereum enabled the rise of Decentralized Applications (DApps), combining smart contracts with user interfaces and sometimes decentralized storage or messaging systems.
Web3 is the vision of an internet built on decentralized protocols, with Ethereum being one of its foundational technologies.
Ethereum's culture emphasizes rapid innovation and experimentation, requiring developers to adapt to frequent changes.
